      "quote": "Although comprehensive, the omission of device-specific and risk-factor terms may have limited retrieval of eligible studies, and citation searching was not undertaken. Future reviews should expand search terms to include specific NCDs, risk factors, and device-specific terminology to ensure broader coverage. ... Included studies were limited to publications from the five years prior to the search, an intentional scope decision aligned with WHO guidance to capture contemporary evidence for POC tests and health literacy definitions. As the final search was conducted in May 2023, the study is limited in its consideration of more recent evidence or developments in POC testing, and the findings should be interpreted as reflecting the evidence base up to that date. Additionally, 11 full-text reports were unable to be retrieved, largely due to being abstract-only or presented in nonwritten formats. While these reports may lack sufficient methodological detail for full appraisal, their exclusion may still introduce retrieval bias and contribute to the narrow evidence base identified. Meta-analysis was not feasible due to variability between interventions, disease focus, measurement tools, and population characteristics. Outcomes were assessed using diverse instruments, ranging from validated tools (e.g. PAM-13) to study-developed questionnaires, qualitative interviews, and clinical markers of adherence or control. This mix of validated and self-reported proxy measures introduces differences in measurement rigour, further limiting comparability across studies. Designs were predominantly cross-sectional or short-term, with limited longitudinal follow-up. Furthermore, equity indicators were inconsistently reported, limiting disaggregated analysis. Although age and sex variables were consistently reported, ethnicity and socioeconomic indicators were often absent, restricting the ability to generalise findings to diverse populations. ... Several studies did not 